    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d1168">

                <pb facs="1276" n="1264" sameAs="frus:frus1944v03/pg1264"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">Moscow Embassy Files, Lot F–96</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">President Roosevelt to
                        the Chairman of the Council of People’s Commissars of the Soviet Union</hi>
                        (<hi rendition="#italic">Stalin</hi>)<anchor
                        n="92" xml:id="d1168-f1"/>
                </head>
                <dateline>
                    <date ana="#date_undated-inferred-from-document-content-and-sibling-dates"
                        calendar="gregorian" notAfter="1944-02-22T23:59:59+03:00"
                        notBefore="1944-02-21T00:00:00-04:00">undated</date>
                </dateline>
                <p>The text of the Prime Minister’s message of Feb. 20 to you on the subject of a
                    tentative settlement of the Polish post-war boundary by an agreement between the
                    Soviet and Polish Governments is known to me.</p>
                <p>If accepted, the Prime Minister’s suggestion goes far toward furthering our
                    prospects of an early defeat of Germany and I am pleased to recommend that you
                    give favorable and sympathetic consideration to it.</p>
                <p>I think, as I intimated before, that the most realistic problem is to be assured
                    that when you get into Poland your armies will be assisted by the Poles.</p>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d1168-f1" n="92" type="source" xml:id="d1168-f1-content">
                <p>On February 22, 1944, Ambassador Harriman telegraphed to President Roosevelt that
                    he would hold up delivery of this message until Stalin returned to Moscow, so
                    that it would be received just before a message from Prime Minister Churchill
                    would be delivered by the British Ambassador, Sir Archibald Clark Kerr. The next
                    day the President telegraphed approval of this postponement. The message was
                    received by Stalin on February 28.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
